/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 2 — THE TRUNK MAINS
   Sewer band. Register: bathroom-horror + implied mutagenic industrial
   waste. Hub = Confluence (in sewer-demons.twee). This file holds the
   layer's spokes: Ascent, Descent, Gate, Rest, 3 Caches, 2 Snares, and
   organic Passage filler. Depths registered in setup.depthOf (= 2).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- ASCENT / DESCENT ---------- */

:: TheRisers [upper]
The risers are a stack of vertical pipe-galleries climbing up out of the trunk
mains, iron ladders bolted between them, the air getting cleaner and colder with
every level until it tastes of rain instead of rot. This is the spine of the
ascent — the way up out of the mains and into the storm drains, the last layer
of sewer before the sky.

Your lungs ease a little, here. Whatever the drums below are leaking, it pools; it
doesn't climb. The galleries stack away above you, each landing a little paler than
the last, the iron of the ladders going from rust-scaled to merely cold to — higher
still, where your light won't quite reach — a dull honest grey that has not had hell
worked into it yet. The brick changes with the air: down here it sweats; up there it
is only old.

Grey thin daylight leaks down the rising main from somewhere above — the
most beautiful thing you have seen since the sack came down — but it only //leaks//,
because the top of the climb is plugged: packed wall to wall, you'll find, with a
fatberg the size of a bus, close enough to ache for and impossible to pass without
getting through the berg first.

<<link "Up the rising main, to the fatberg that chokes it">><<goto "TheFatberg">><</link>>
<<link "Aside, off the gallery, where something clear is glinting">><<goto "TheCleanPool">><</link>>
<<link "Back down to the black valve">><<goto "TheBlackValve">><</link>>


:: TheSiphon [upper]
A worn stone stair drops away from the trunk mains into a dark that smells
older — less ammonia, less chemistry, more the cold mineral breath of something
sealed a very long time. The poisons of the upper drains don't seem to reach down
there. Whatever runs below runs by different rules, and they are not kinder ones.

A few steps down and the brickwork of the mains gives out behind you, and the wall
becomes dressed stone — block laid on block by hands that knew their trade, the
mortar between them worn to a hollow you could lay a finger in, the treads dished by
a traffic that stopped before the city above had a name. Someone went up and down
this stair, many times, for years, and then nobody did. The cold rises to meet you
the way water meets a sinking weight.

This is the way deeper — first into the old drains the city built its trunk mains
on top of, and then, somewhere past those, the drowned places and the mouth's own
gut. You don't have to take it. Almost nobody who takes it says they meant to.

<<link "Down the stair, into the old drains">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE ---------- */

:: TheBlackValve [upper]
The way up to the risers runs through a low gallery flooded to the chest with
something that is not water: black, oily, dead still, a skin on it that holds the
shape of your lamplight a beat too long after you move. A single drip of it on
your boot has already eaten a pale crater into the leather. To reach the risers,
you have to get through it.

<<if $valveClosed>>
But the gallery is draining — you shut the valve upstream, and the black tide is
sinking past your knees, your ankles, gone, leaving only a greasy tideline on the
brick and a smell that will follow you for days. You cross on almost-dry ground.

<<link "Through to the risers">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the weir">><<goto "TheWeir">><</link>>
<<else>>
The valve-house upstream might drain it, if you can work the seized wheel. Or you
can simply wade — and let whatever lives in the black water take its bite of you.

<<link "Wade through the black water (it costs you)">>
  <<corrupt "legL" "filth" 15>>
  <<goto "TheRisers">>
<</link>>
<<link "Go back and try the valve-house first">><<goto "TheValvePuzzle">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the weir">><<goto "TheWeir">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: ThePumpRoom [upper]
Off the sump there is a dry room — actually dry, the first dry floor since the
sack — where the old lift-pumps sit cold and enormous under a film of honest dust
instead of slime. The air is merely stale, not poisonous. The door still shuts.

Deeper in, the room is a quiet cathedral of dead machinery: the pumps rise on their
plinths in a row, painted a green that has only faded and not rotted, their great
casings stencilled with serial numbers and a maker's name and the date of a last
inspection that someone, once, was paid to come down and sign. A chair is tipped over
by the control board. A jacket hangs on a hook. Whoever kept this room walked out of
it one ordinary shift and was simply never replaced, and the dust has been settling,
politely, undisturbed, ever since.

You put your back to a machine that has not turned in fifty years and, for a
little while, you are nobody's prey. The shaking in your hands settles. The
shallow burns and the chemical stings stop mattering quite so much. You breathe.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "A clean-looking basin, through the far door">><<goto "TheBackflow">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES / PUZZLE ---------- */

:: TheGritTrap [upper]
The grit trap is a sunken bay where the flow slows and drops its heavy load — sand,
stone, and a century of whatever the city poured down a drain and wished away, all of
it settled into a grey bank that slopes down into water you can't see the bottom of.
The smell here is mineral, gritty, almost clean compared to the mains — the smell of
the river the sewer used to be.

Step to the edge of the bank and the load resolves into its century of contents: not
just sand but the silt of a thousand spilled pockets, a sediment of small lost things
pressed flat by the weight of all the grit above them. Half-glimpsed in it: rings,
coins, a wristwatch stopped at a time that mattered to someone. You can dig, if you
don't mind what your hands come up wearing.

<<if $gritLooted>>
You've already picked the trap over. The rest is just grit and old sorrow.
<<else>>
Half-glimpsed in the heavy load the flow drops here: rings, coin, a wristwatch stopped
at a time that mattered to someone — diggable, if you come back this way and don't mind
what your hands come up wearing.
<</if>>

<<if not $sifterTalked>>
A man sits in the heavy load, arms grey to the shoulder in it, sorting it handful by patient handful,
and does not startle when your light finds him.
<<linkreplace "Ask him what he's looking for">><<nobr>><<set $sifterTalked to true>>
//Found a watch yet?// he says, not looking up. //Stopped at a time that mattered to someone. They
all are.// His hands do not pause. //You'll dig past a hundred and not feel the one where you stopped
mattering too — that's the trick of it, friend. There's no clean line, no step where the man ends and
the sorting starts. You just look up one day and your hands are still going, and you can't think why
you'd want them to quit.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The sifter sorts on, past speech now, his hands going of their own accord; he has nothing left to say
that his hands aren't already saying.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>


:: TheLostConduit [upper]
A conduit the flow abandoned — silted and dry-ish, the current long since rerouted
somewhere upstream so that this run is left as a dead backwater, a pocket where things
wash in on the rare high water and never wash back out. The air is still and dusty and
oddly peaceful, the loudest thing in it the small grit-fall of silt settling that your
footsteps disturb.

The longer you stand the more the silt gives up its catch: shapes humped under the grey
that the dim light slowly sorts into objects, the slow tide-line of everything the mains
ever lost reaching this far and stopping. Half-buried in it — a maintenance kit, a coil
of something, and the patient bones of a previous optimist, arranged just so where they
came to rest. Worth a look, if your nerve holds.

<<if $conduitLooted>>
You've stripped it of anything that spends. The bones you leave where they lie.
<<else>>
Half-buried in the grey silt is coin, among a maintenance kit and a coil of something
and the patient bones of a previous optimist — worth a search, if your nerve holds the
trip back for it.
<</if>>

<<if not $optimistTalked>>
One set of the bones is fresher than the rest — propped against the conduit wall as if it sat down to
wait — and it is, you realize, still talking, cheerful, as your light finds it.
<<linkreplace "Hear out the cheerful one">><<nobr>><<set $optimistTalked to true>>
//Don't mind the bones,// it says, grinning, silt in the grin. //Previous fella. No head for the work,
not like us. Me, I'm just topping off — found a dry run that cuts the whole berg out, straight up, no
grease, no toll. Show you, even, soon as I've grabbed that last coil. Been meaning to. It's right
there.// It does not move toward it. It has not, you suspect, moved toward anything in a very long
time. //In and out, that's me. Always just leaving.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
It's still going to grab that last coil any minute, the same grin, the same silt in it. The shortcut's
still right there. It still doesn't move.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the rat-run">><<goto "TheRatRun">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the grit chamber">><<goto "GritChamber">><</link>>


:: TheValvePuzzle [upper]
The valve-house is a closet of seized iron, no wider than a coffin stood on end: a
great wheel-valve furred orange with rust dominates the cramped dark, and around it
the walls bristle with pipework, every run of it sweating, every joint of it weeping
a slow rust-tea down into a drain you cannot see but can hear.

When your light steadies you find the wheel is ringed by a maze of smaller cocks and
bleeds, a dozen of them, each labelled on a little brass plate in a copperplate hand
the damp has half-erased — OPEN FIRST, and an arrow; CAUTION; a sequence of numbers
gone to green verdigris. Someone laid this out to be read by a calm man with time and
a manual, and you are neither, and the man who was is long gone wherever the works men
go.

Turning the wheel cold would shear the spindle, you can see that much — it
has to be done in order: bleed the pressure here, crack that cock, let the line sigh
itself empty, then the wheel will turn. The house waits for you to get it right or to
get it wrong, with the patience of iron, which has nothing else to do.

<<if $valveClosed>>
The wheel is shut. Somewhere downstream, you can hear the black gallery draining
away into wherever such things go to wait.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Work the bleeds in order, then turn the wheel">>
<<set $valveClosed to true>>
The line sighs, shudders, and goes slack. Downstream, with a sound like a long
slow swallow, the black gallery begins to drain. You have made the way up passable
without paying for it in skin.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the weir">><<goto "TheWeir">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheStallRow [upper]
The north pipe opens onto something that does not belong in a sewer: a row of
lavatory stalls, white once, doors hanging, the porcelain inside crazed and brown.
A maintenance crew must have used them, a lifetime ago. Most doors stand open on
empty filth.

Two are not like the rest. One, at the very end, is shut, and a slow tongue of black
water spreads from under it. And one, partway along the row, has an eerie pink glow
coming from inside it — steady, sourceless, the exact wrong colour for anywhere down here.

The pink light pulls at you the way a lit window pulls at someone out in the cold. You could
go down the row and look inside.
<<linkreplace "Go to the pink-lit stall and look inside">>
<<set _freeStall to (random(1, 100) lte 20)>>
<<if not _freeStall>>
Up close, the stall's door is pushed-to, not hanging open like the others, and the glow leaks
around its edges in a thin warm seam — and there are feet under it, and the small private unhurried
sounds of something using a latrine for its purpose. It's occupied. You are not the kind of fool who
hauls open a glowing door in hell; the deep, it seems, queues for the privy like anything else. You
could come back when it's free.
<<elseif $magicKnown>>
Up close, the stall stands open and empty, and the glow is rising straight up out of the toilet hole,
warm and pink and steady, lighting a roll of paper on the bent wire arm. You carry the Word already;
you know what that light is now. You tear off a few sheets for their honest first purpose, and the
printed script lies dead under your hand, just paper, a joke the deep has stopped telling you.
<<elseif setup.canReadGrimoire()>>
Up close, the stall stands open and empty, and the glow is rising straight up out of the toilet hole —
and lit by it, on the bent wire arm, a roll of toilet paper, and the paper is //printed//. Not a
pattern. Writing — the same close wet not-language the deep cuts into its gates, and the pink light
comes up //through// the script from the hole below, lighting it like something is reading along under
the floor. A foot of scripture a sheet, hung in a privy at the end of the world, set exactly where a
frightened animal would sit alone with nothing to do but read. You know this light, now; you know what
it lights.

<<linkreplace "Sit, and read the roll — one sheet, then the next">><<nobr>>
<<run setup.learnMagic("grimoire")>>
You sit, because that is what the stall is for and what the roll is for, and you read — a
sheet, and the next, and the next — and reading it is mouthing it, and mouthing it is
//speaking// it, and somewhere between one square of scripture and the next the words stop
being on the paper and start being in you. The deep printed its book on the thing it knew a
captive would pick up in an idle, undefended moment, and you have picked it up, and now the
well is open behind your eyes, cold and full. You can speak the deep's tongue. You will pay
for every sentence of it — but you can speak it. You pocket the rest of the roll. You may, you
think, want to read ahead.
//(you have learned the Word — Mana now shows in your kit; in a fight, the Cast action calls it)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
Up close, the stall stands open and empty, and the glow is rising straight up out of the toilet hole,
lighting a roll of paper on the bent wire arm. The light unsettles you — a toilet should not shine,
least of all that colour — and the paper looks printed with something, a fault in the manufacture
you'd guess, ink bled through the pulp. But a glowing hole in a hell-privy is exactly the kind of
thing a person learns, very fast, not to investigate; you take what you need from the lit roll,
keeping your eyes up, and back out. //(There is more to that pink light than you'll see on your own —
a sharper eye, or a word from someone who knows what the glow means, might change that.)//
<</if>>
<</linkreplace>>

The shut stall at the end you keep well clear of. Every privy is a place where a person is alone and
undefended, and the old demons knew it best — the lurker of the latrine, the thing that takes you with
your guard and your trousers both down. Whatever waits behind that shut door has waited longer than
the sewer is old, for exactly one curious, solitary fool; to open it would, near as you can tell, be
the last thing you ever did.
<<link "Open the shut stall anyway — and meet what's been waiting">><<goto "EndingTakenStall">><</link>>

<<link "Leave the stalls. Quickly.">><<goto "GritChamberCleared">><</link>>


:: TheBackflow [upper]
Through the far door of the pump-room is a thing you have stopped daring to hope
for: clean water. A basin of it — clear, still, faintly steaming, fed from some
pipe that must run up to the world, to before. After everything the drains have
worked into your skin, the urge to strip and scrub yourself raw in it is almost
more than you can stand.

Something about it is too clean. Too warm. Too exactly the thing you wanted most.
But God, you want it.

<<linkreplace "Wash. Scrub the filth off — all of it.">>
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 30>>
You wash, and it is bliss — and under the grime your skin comes away too, the top
layer of you rinsing into the basin with the dirt, and what's left feels cleaner
and thinner and not entirely your own. The water, you notice as you rise, is no
dirtier than when you began. It did not take the filth. It took the other thing.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back away from the basin">>
  <<soothe 2>>
  <<goto "ThePumpRoom">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheScreeningHall [upper]
A long hall of bar-screens, most collapsed, where the flow was once combed of its
solids. Now the screens comb it of nothing, and the solids walk. The floor is a
grating over a void that breathes cold up at you, and along one wall a bank of
warning-lights — all dead but one, which pulses a slow contaminated amber over
everything, patient as a heartbeat.

<<if not setup.has("pipe")>>
One of the collapsed screens has shed a length of its steel — a rod of it, man-high, heavy and
cold and blunt, the kind of thing that would stave a skull, or scatter something that hasn't got
a skull to stave.
<<linkreplace "Pry the screen-bar loose — a length of pipe">>
<<acquire "pipe">>
You work it free. It is a good honest weight in your hands, and the having of it changes how the
dark feels, a little, in your favour.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<if $screeningGolemCleared>>
The thing that stood up out of the screened sludge lies scattered back across the grating — so much
spread filth again, already, slowly, patiently beginning to gather itself back toward the shape it
briefly wore. You do not wait to watch it finish.
<<else>>
And then the solids walk. The sludge banked against the dead screens shifts, and gathers, and
//stands// — a shit-golem, a sewage demon, the filth of the whole hall swirled up into one slow
enormous will, far more than the sum of the muck that makes it. It hauls itself off the screens and
comes for you, and you understand at a glance that a blade would only part it and let it close again.

<<scentof "shitgolem">>
<<link "Put the sewage demon down">>
  <<run setup.startFight("shitgolem", "TheScreeningHallCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "The grit chamber, off the end">><<goto "GritChamber">><</link>>
<<link "The grit trap, below the screens">><<goto "TheGritTrap">><</link>>
<<link "A manhole, set in the far wall">><<goto "TheManholeBase">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


:: TheScreeningHallCleared [upper]
<<set $screeningGolemCleared to true>>
The sewage demon comes apart the way they do when you scatter them right — not killed, because there
was never quite anything there to kill, only //dispersed//, flung back across the grating as the
loose filth it was always going to settle into again. It will reconvene, given a patient while. You
intend to be a long way up by then.

<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE CLEAN POOL — the game's only CLEANSE, and you have to win it (owner) ----------
   A single-use clear pool off the risers (reachable before the fatberg is burned open). You
   discover it at the same instant shit-golems do, and it is a RACE: fight them off and the pool
   stays clean (wash → soothe, single use); let them reach it and the filth fouls it (drinking
   raises corruption instead). State machine in $cleanPool ("new"/"won"/"fouled"); the "fight"
   choice pre-sets "fouled" so a flee/loss leaves it fouled without touching generic CombatLost —
   only the victory lander (TheCleanPoolWon) flips it to "won". */

:: TheCleanPool [upper]
<<if $cleanPool == "new">>
Off the rising-main gallery, where a cracked storm-inlet lets a thread of upper water down before
the mains can get to it, a pool has gathered in a basin of clean concrete — and it is //clear//.
Actually clear. The first clear water you have seen since the rain-shaft, holding the grey daylight
from above like something remembered, smelling of nothing, of rain, of the world. After everything
you have waded and breathed and been made to drink, the sight of it goes through you like grief.

You are not the only one who has found it. Across the basin the sludge of the mains is moving —
gathering, banking, hauling itself up off the floor in the slow swirl you have learned to dread:
shit-golems, two of them, sensing the clean the way rot senses a wound, sludging toward the basin to
//reclaim// it, to fold it back into the foul. It is a race, and you both arrived at once. If you
can put them down before they reach the water, the pool stays clean, and yours. If they get to it
first, it will be just one more thing the deep has spoiled.

<<scentof `["shitgolem","shitgolem"]`>>
<<link "Get between the golems and the water — fight for the pool">>
  <<set $cleanPool to "fouled">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["shitgolem", "shitgolem"], "TheCleanPoolWon")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Hang back — you are in no shape to take two of them">>
  <<set $cleanPool to "fouled">>
  <<goto "TheCleanPool">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the risers">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>

<<elseif $cleanPool == "won">>
The basin is yours. The two golems lie scattered back across the gallery floor as the loose filth
they were, already beginning their patient gather, but they will not reconvene before you are done
here — and the pool, between them and you, stayed //clean//. It holds the daylight, clear to the
concrete at its bottom, and it is the one uncorrupted thing in all the miles you have come.

<<if not $cleanPoolUsed>>
<<linkreplace "Kneel, and wash the deep off you in the clear water">>
<<run setup.soothe(30)>><<set $cleanPoolUsed to true>>
You kneel, and cup it, and pour it over your head and your arms and the back of your neck, and it is
cold and clean and it //takes//. The grime lifts; the grey sloughs; the crawling under your skin
quiets and recedes, washed thin and carried off down the cracked drain — not all of it, the deepest
of it has set too far to rinse, but more than you dared hope, more than anything down here was ever
supposed to give back. You come up dripping and lighter and very nearly yourself, and for one whole
breath you are clean.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You have washed here already; the basin is drawn down to a clean, ordinary nothing now, and what it
took off you, it took for good. There is no second washing. You did not expect there to be.
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the risers">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>

<<else>>
The golems reached it first. By the time you got between them they were already in the basin, slumped
and dissolving into it on purpose, giving the clean water back the filth it had briefly escaped — and
now the pool is the colour of the mains again, a grey swirling broth with the daylight curdled out of
it, and the rot-smell rising off it where, a moment ago, there was rain. It is fouled. Of course it is.
The deep does not let a clean thing stay.

<<linkreplace "Drink anyway — you are so thirsty, and it was almost clean">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(12, 18)>><<corrupt _seg "filth" _amt>>
You drink, because the memory of how clear it was is stronger than the sight of what it is now, and it
is the deep itself going down — thick and grey and patient — and it does not slake anything. It only
adds to you, settles its swirl into one more part of you, the fouled pool finding, like everything
down here, one more thing to be the inside of.
<</linkreplace>>
<<link "Leave the fouled water">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheCleanPoolWon [upper]
<<set $cleanPool to "won">>
You get between them and the water — and a length of blunt steel, or your own two fists, does to a
shit-golem the one thing that works: it //scatters// them, flings them back across the gallery as the
loose muck they are, before either can reach the basin and slump itself in. They will gather again,
patiently, the way they do. But not in time. The pool, behind you, stayed clear the whole while.

<<link "To the pool you kept clean">><<goto "TheCleanPool">><</link>>


:: TheFatberg [upper]
<<roomart "fatberg">>
The rising main — the last climb before the storm drains and the grey daylight above
them — is throttled all the way shut by a fatberg the size of a bus: a grey-white reef
of congealed grease and wet-wipes and the slow geology of everything the city flushed
and forgot, packed wall to wall, sealing the way up. It sweats a clear oil that pools
and catches your lamp. Set into it like a fly in old amber is the wet bulk of the thing
that has made it home.

<<if $fatbergBurned>>
Where the fatberg was there is now a charred and dripping hole, burned clean through, the
grease gone to greasy smoke and the way up open at last. Cold rain-smell pours down through it.

<<link "Up through the burned-out main, into the storm drains">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>
<<elseif setup.has("brand")>>
The whole reef is grease, and grease burns — and the branch in your fist is a fire that lasts.
You could set it to the berg, and not stop to wonder what burning a bus-sized plug of flammable
filth in a closed pipe does to the air in a closed pipe.

<<linkreplace "Set the branch that never stops burning to the fatberg">>
<<set $fatbergBurned to true>>
You touch the cold blue branch to the sweating grease and the grease takes it the way grease
takes a flame — all at once, with a soft enormous //whump// that pulls the air from your lungs
and a heat that drives you back down the gallery — and the fatberg burns. It burns a long, filthy,
roaring while, eating the bus-sized plug down to a charred and dripping hole, and when it finally
dies back there is a way up where there was none, and rain-smell coming down it, and the branch
in your fist burning on exactly as before, undiminished, already cool again in your hand.

<<link "Up through the burned-out main, into the storm drains">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You set your shoulder to it and it gives nothing; you claw at it and come away foul to the elbow.
It is packed wall to wall and it is not moving. The grease would //burn// — you can smell exactly
how it would burn — if you had a fire that would last long enough to catch it. You don't. Not yet.
Nothing you've found down here holds a flame for more than a breath.
<</if>>

<<link "The narrow gut, into the fatberg">><<goto "Gut">><</link>>
<<link "A drum-store, off the side passage">><<goto "TheDrumStore">><</link>>
<<link "Back down to the risers">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


:: TheDrumStore [upper]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 2>>
A side chamber stacked with steel drums, most split and slumped, their contents
long since wept across the floor in stains that have eaten the concrete down to
bare rebar. The air in here is different the instant you cross the threshold —
heavier, sweetish, with a chemical edge that sits on the tongue like the smell of a
hospital gone wrong. Your eyes water. At the back of your sinuses, something faintly
begins to itch, and to change.

The drums go back further than your light: rank on rank of them into the dark, some
upright and bulging, some folded flat where their seams let go, all of them bleeding
into a single shared slick that has found the low corner of the floor and made a
black pond there. The stencils have rotted to a few legible letters — a partial word,
a hazard diamond bleached to a ghost, a lot-number — enough to know none of this was
ever meant to be down here, that someone tipped it down a manhole one quiet night
rather than pay to have it taken away honest, and that to breathe it is a slow
mistake.

You should not stay. You know you should not stay; the room itself is telling you, in
the only language it has, that everything it touches it keeps. But the slick on the
floor catches your lamp in oily rainbows, almost pretty, and the longer you stand the
less your eyes water, which is not the room growing kinder. It is you growing used to
it.

<<if not $tarmanDealt>>
Among the split drums something shifts that you took for another stain: a man who came down for the
drums and stayed too long breathing them, gone slick and black-streaked, more of the leaked chemistry
now than of himself. He has been stripping the dead who fell here, and he has their coin, and he will
deal.
<<linkreplace "Take the dead men's coin off the Tarman — your skin for their purse">><<nobr>><<set $tarmanDealt to true>><<set _c to random(14, 22)>><<run setup.gainCoins(_c)>>
He pours it into your hands, warm from him, more than you have held down here — and as you lean in to
take it the fumes come with it, into your eyes and your sinuses and the soft wet back of your throat,
and something there that was holding out stops holding out. //Spends the same up there as down,// he
says, sinking back among his drums, //if you get up there. I had a purse like that once.//
//(+<<= _c>> coins)//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 22>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The Tarman has gone back to being mostly tar, settled among the drums he is becoming a part of; he
gave you the coin, and what's left of him has nothing else you would carry out.
<</if>>

<<link "Out, before you breathe more">><<goto "TheFatberg">><</link>>


:: TheWeir [upper]
The main drops over a low concrete weir into a stilling basin, and the sound reaches
you before the room does — a flat, ceaseless, throat-clearing roar, the noise of a
great deal of bad water falling a short way forever. The water that goes over it is
the colour of a bad bruise, foaming yellow-white where it breaks, and the foam does
not pop the way honest foam pops; it heaps, and slides, and sails the basin in slow
yellow rafts that go round and round and never reach a shore.

Closer, the weir is a tidy thing in all this rot — poured straight, its crest still
true, a painted scale-board bolted to the wall beside it where someone once read the
flow off in honest feet and inches. The paint is gone to a few flaked numerals now,
but the board is still there, still doing its mute job, gauging a grade nobody up top
will ever come down to act on. The spray off the falling water hangs in your light as
a fine sour mist, and settles on your skin, and does not wash off so much as soak in.

Beyond the basin the pipe climbs — you can feel the air pulling that way, up toward a
cold that isn't hell's cold, a draught with the faintest thread of the real world in
it. The risers are up there. So is whatever the black valve is holding back, and the
nearer you stand to the climb, the more clearly you understand that the one bars the
other.

<<if not $gaugerTalked>>
A man in the rotted remains of a works jacket leans on the weir-rail, watching the foam break with
the unbothered air of someone exactly where his job is.
<<linkreplace "Ask him how long he's worked the weir">><<nobr>><<set $gaugerTalked to true>>
//Foam's gone yellow again — that's the line off, up past the third manhole, and nobody coming to
bleed it but me.// He says it the way a man says it's going to rain. //I mind the weir, the grade,
the seized valve yonder they sent me down for. Honest works job. You get used to the smell faster than
you'd credit — a week, maybe. After that it's just the air.// He nods at nothing in particular. //And
that's when you've gone, friend. Not the day you came down. The day the stink quit reaching you. I'd
say clock off, were I you. Only I never worked out how, and I held the keys.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
He's watching the foam again, reading the grade, on a shift he can't get off; he nods, once, the way
you nod at a man on the same job, and says no more.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the risers — but the black valve bars the way">><<goto "TheBlackValve">><</link>>
<<link "A valve-house, off the basin">><<goto "TheValvePuzzle">><</link>>
<<link "A side sluice, half-choked">><<goto "TheSluice">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


:: TheManholeBase [upper]
A manhole shaft climbs the far wall — a narrow brick chimney barely wider than your
shoulders, rungs intact, and at the top of it, small and round and impossibly far,
daylight. Real daylight, the grey of an overcast afternoon, so close your chest aches
with it. You can smell it: cut grass, wet tarmac, rain on warm pavement, the whole
ordinary world breathing down the shaft at you through a coin of sky.

You climb. The rungs are sound, rust-furred but sound, and the daylight grows as you
go — not much, the disc never gets large, but it gets near, near enough at the top
that you could fit your eye to the gap at the cover's rim and watch a cloud go by. You
get a hand to the cover and push, and push, and it does not move: tarred shut from
above, or parked under three tons of something, or simply never meant to open for the
likes of what comes up out of here.

You climb back down, slower than you went up, the daylight shrinking to a coin again
above you, and at the bottom you stand a moment in the dark with your face still
turned up at it. The light watches you do it, and says nothing. It has watched a great
many people do exactly this, you suspect, and it has never once let any of them
through.

<<if not $voiceHeard>>
You are about to give it up when a sound stops you: a voice, on the world-side of the cover,
close as a confessional — someone who works the surface, who heard you push.
<<linkreplace "Put your mouth to the seam and call up through the cover">><<nobr>><<set $voiceHeard to true>>
//Don't,// the voice says, before you can ask. //Don't push it. We tar them shut from up here on
purpose — every cover on the network, since we worked out what comes up if we don't.// A pause; the
scrape of someone not quite leaving. //We know what's down there. We've always known. We just decided
it was cheaper to seal it in than to go down after it. You understand? Nobody's coming. There was
never going to be anyone coming. That's the budget.// And the feet move off, unhurried, a person
walking away from a drain.
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 4>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You put your ear to the seam again, but there is no one now — only the wind across the cover, or a
voice so like the wind you can no longer be sure you aren't asking it to be there.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>


:: TheRatRun [upper]
A low crawl-tunnel off the east wall, barely shoulder-width, so that you go in bent
double and then on your hands. The brick is warm under your palms — warmer than brick
has any business being down here — and it twitches, faintly, continuously, with the
traffic of vermin moving through the walls and over the ceiling and, you realize, very
close behind you.

The deeper you crawl the more the tunnel is *theirs*. The mortar is packed with shed
fur and gnawed bone and the small grease of many bodies passing the same way for many
years; nests bulge from every crack, and droppings carpet the floor you are putting
your hands in. They are watching from the dark ahead, dozens of them, low to the
ground, their eyes giving back your light in wet pairs — and they do not scatter the
way honest rats do. The mutated rats den in here, fat and wrong and many, and they
hold their ground, and something about the way they hold it tells you they have eaten
crawlers like you before, and found nothing in it to fear.

Past the massed eyes the tunnel goes on into the warm dark, and there is the mouth of a
silted conduit, and the dull glint of things worth having in the silt. The deeper run
keeps going past it, too, into a heat and a hush you have no wish to crawl toward —
something down there owns this place, and the rats are only its household.

<<scentof "rat">>
<<link "Push through the rats toward the conduit">>
  <<run setup.startFight("rat", "TheLostConduit")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Deeper in, to where the run opens out and the big ones den">><<goto "TheRatKing">><</link>>
<<link "Back out to the confluence">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>


:: TheRatKing [upper]
The rat-run opens, without warning, into a chamber it had no business hiding: a den, high and dry
and warm, lit by nothing you can find and yet somehow not quite dark, its walls a woven thatch of
gnawed cable and bone and stolen cloth, layered and patched and *built*, the work of patient hands
over patient years.

And it is not rats that keep it. It is rat-//men//, the mutation finished, upright and handed and
unhurried, a dozen of them at least sitting the den's edges like a council — and as your light moves
over them you see how settled they are, how at home, grooming, murmuring, one of them working a
strip of cloth between clever black fingers, a household going about its evening with you standing in
the door of it. At the heart of it, on a heaped throne of matted nest, a vast pale bloated thing they
tend and feed: the rat-king, the den's swollen mother-father, gone too heavy to move, ringed by its
grown children.

Then, as one, every wet clever face turns to you, and the murmuring stops, and the only sound is the
small wet working of the king's breath on its throne. You are the thing that came up out of the run.
You are not, the silence makes plain, the first to come up out of the run, only the latest, and the
den knows exactly what to do with the latest. One of them stands. Two of the den's dog-sized
rats come off the walls with it, unhurried, flanking wide — a household attending its champion,
because a den that has done this before does not send anything alone.

<<if $ratKingCleared>>
The one that stood lies where you put it, and the council has drawn back to the den's dark edges,
watching, unwilling to be the next to try you, and the rat-king on its nest regards you out of its
fat patience with something almost like respect. You are not welcome here. But you are, for now, not
food.

<<link "Back out to the rat-run">><<goto "TheRatRun">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<scentof `["ratman","rat","rat"]`>>
<<link "Put the rising rat-man down before the rest decide">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["ratman", "rat", "rat"], "TheRatKingCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back out, slowly, before the council decides for you">><<goto "TheRatRun">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheRatKingCleared [upper]
<<set $ratKingCleared to true>>
You put the rat-man down in the middle of its own council — it and the flanking pair both — and
the den goes very still — a dozen clever faces running a dozen small sums at once — and then, as one, the rest draw back to the edges
and let you be, because the den's arithmetic has changed and you are, for the moment, on the wrong
side of it to eat. The rat-king watches you from its nest-throne and does not blink, and you back
out the way you came with the skin crawling all down your spine.

<<link "Back to the den">><<goto "TheRatKing">><</link>>


:: TheSluice [upper]
A side sluice, low and narrow and half-choked with a grey felt of fungus that has
found something in the runoff it likes. It grows on everything — the walls, the dead
penstock, the very air seems thick with the spore of it — a soft pale pelt that
deadens your footsteps and your voice and the drip of water until the silence in here
has a texture, woolen, close, like a room hung floor to ceiling in old felt.

When your light travels the walls you see the felt is not one thing but a slow
colony — paler and older toward the dead end, where it has had longest to work, ridged
and lobed and faintly breathing as the draught moves through it, hung here and there
with little beads of clear liquor it has wept out of itself. The sluice dead-ends at a
penstock rusted immovably shut, and the felt has grown clean over the seized wheel of
it, swallowing the iron the way it has swallowed everything else that stopped moving.

There is nothing here but the fungus, and the strong, specific sense that the fungus
is aware of you — not as a thing watches a thing, but as still water is aware of a
finger laid on its surface: a pressure, a noting, an unhurried registering of the new
arrival. It has all the time there is. It is in no particular hurry about you at all.

<<if not $feltNoticed>>
You feel its attention the way you feel a held gaze across a room, and when you hold still the grey
felt seems, very slowly, to lean.
<<linkreplace "Hold still, and let the felt finish noticing you">><<nobr>><<set $feltNoticed to true>>
It has tasted everything the runoff ever carried down — years of it, the whole drowned census of the
mains, soaked up and kept and slowly understood. And it tastes you now: the change in you, the thing
the water has started, the way you'd notice a season turning, with no alarm at all. It is in no hurry.
It has met what you are becoming a thousand times, and already knows how the tasting ends. It needs to
do nothing but wait, and be tasted back, and agree.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The felt has gone back to its slow attention, which was only ever attention; there is nothing in it
for you now but the certainty that it has your measure, and all the time there is to take it.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the weir">><<goto "TheWeir">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-2 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingTakenStall [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("taken")>>
You open the door, because some part of you always opens the door. What is folded
into the stall is mostly mouth, and it has waited in the dark of a forgotten
latrine for exactly one curious, solitary fool — for longer than the sewer above
it has existed, for longer than the city, for as long as people have gone alone
and undefended into the dark to do what bodies do.

It does not hurry. There is no one to hear you, and nowhere to go but down — into
the black water, into the patient wet dark where the privy-things keep what the
careless hand them.

//You answered the door. Something had been waiting behind it a very long time.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>


/* ---------- DEMON BARGAIN (Tallow the Gorger; reached from TheFatberg) ---------- */

:: Gut [upper]
<<set $metTallow to true>>
<<nobr>>
The passage narrows to a wet throat of brick, low enough that you go in stooped, the
walls pressing close and warm and faintly sticky, until you are less walking a tunnel
than swallowed into one. Half-blocking the throat ahead is a fatberg — a vast pale
boulder of congealed grease and the city's flushed sins, sweating, faintly sweet,
glistening where your lamp finds it.

Up close the berg is not a single mass but a slow accretion, layer on greasy layer,
and things are set into it like fruit in a terrible suet: a comb, a doll's arm, a
phone, a wedding ring, a hand — and one of the pale lumps, you understand with a lurch,
is breathing. It is wedged into the grease and indistinguishable from it at first,
and then it opens an eye in the suet and the suet is its face. Tallow: a Gorger, one of
the things that grow on what the world discards.

It does not stir to threaten you; it has no need. It fills the throat ahead the way the
berg fills the pipe, settled and enormous and patient, and the warmth you took for the
tunnel's is coming off it, the slow body-heat of a thing that does nothing but
digest. It does not want your secrets. It wants meat, and it has so much to give in
trade.
<</nobr>>

//Hungry?// it asks, kindly. //Cold? I can make you hard to kill. Costs only a
little of what you're made of.//

<<link "Take the ward — let Tallow render a piece of you into armor">>
  <<set $hasWard to true>>
  <<corrupt "armR" "filth" 25>>
  <<goto "TheFatberg">>
<</link>>
<<link "Refuse Tallow outright">>
  <<soothe 6>>
  <<goto "TheFatberg">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- HUB + the grit-chamber fight (moved here from core) ---------- */

:: Confluence [upper]
<<nobr>>
The throat of the hellmouth lets you out into something that almost passes for
ordinary: the trunk mains, brick-arched and mortared, the great pipes the city
laid when it still believed in carrying its waste politely away. But the water
here runs a colour no honest water runs — a thin chemical green that foams where
it falls — and the rust has eaten through the iron to something underneath that
weeps. A rat watches you from a ledge. It has too many eyes, and it is not
troubled that you noticed.
<</nobr>>

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You have stopped minding the smell, and the part of you that has started to find
it almost comfortable is the only thing down here that still frightens you.
<<elseif $corruption gte 35>>
There's a taste of metal and ammonia at the back of your throat that wasn't there
when you came down, and a rash on your forearm you don't remember earning.
<<else>>
This is the way up — it must be. The air moves, and moving air means a surface
somewhere above. You try not to touch the walls.
<</if>>

The mains branch off in every direction, with the logic of a thing that grew
rather than a thing that was planned.

<<if $deepTurns gte setup.SEAL_WARN and not $sealed>>
And you have been turning in them longer than getting out should take. The
branches are starting to repeat — or you are — and that is worse than any smell.
You should pick a way up and *commit* to it, while the choosing is still yours.
<</if>>

<<link "The screening hall, where the rakes are">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>
<<link "Up the south main, to the fatberg that chokes the way out">><<goto "TheFatberg">><</link>>
<<link "A low rat-run off the east wall">><<goto "TheRatRun">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the weir and the risers — the way up">><<goto "TheWeir">><</link>>
<<link "A dry pump-room, off the sump">><<goto "ThePumpRoom">><</link>>
<<link "Down the worn stair, into the lower dark">><<goto "TheSiphon">><</link>>
<<link "Back down the throat, toward the hellmouth">><<goto "Hellmouth">><</link>>


:: GritChamber [upper]
The chamber opens around a dead machine — the screen-rake that once dragged the
solids from the flow, its long corroded screw seized in a trough of grey sludge
that steams faintly and should not, at this temperature, be steaming at all. The
rake's teeth are hung with a beard of rag and root and something that has begun,
very slowly, to grow back toward the light of your lamp.

<<if not $gritRatCleared>>
Something the size of a dog has made a nest in the gearbox, and as your light
finds it, it comes out — a demon rat, hairless and wet and swollen wrong, with
too many eyes and far too much interest in you.

<<scentof "rat">>
<<link "Fight the rat in the gearbox">>
  <<run setup.startFight("rat", "GritChamberCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>
<<else>>
The gearbox nest is quiet now, its tenant dealt with. Pipes run off the chamber
in every direction, stencilled with hazard-labels too furred with grime to read.

<<link "A row of maintenance stalls, off the north pipe">><<goto "TheStallRow">><</link>>
<<link "A flooded conduit, off the south pipe">><<goto "TheLostConduit">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: GritChamberCleared [upper]
/* lands here after winning the gearbox fight; sets the flag, then folds back */
<<set $gritRatCleared to true>>
You drag the thing out of the gearbox by its tail — it comes apart a little in
your hand, more rot than rat — and leave it for whatever eats such things down
here. The nest is yours, for what that's worth.

<<link "A row of maintenance stalls, off the north pipe">><<goto "TheStallRow">><</link>>
<<link "A flooded conduit, off the south pipe">><<goto "TheLostConduit">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the screening hall">><<goto "TheScreeningHall">><</link>>
